Your "+1 to one element" insight is actually the clearest way to understand what's happening. You're creating frequency collisions — when two input values map to the same output value, one output value becomes rarer and another becomes more common, reducing entropy. Your PRNG does this across all 256 bytes simultaneously with just 2 bytes of description overhead.

The limitation is that XOR is only one family of collision-creating transforms. Here are concrete things to try:

1. Additive mod instead of XOR
XOR on uniform data preserves the distribution perfectly. Additive mod 256 has different collision behavior:


// encode
Data[x] = (Data[x] + getRandomNum(seed - x, tr + x)) % 256;
// decode  
Data[x] = (Data[x] - getRandomNum(seed - x, tr + x) + 256) % 256;
Try both and take whichever gives lower entropy.

2. Widen the parameter space
You currently search 255×254 ≈ 65K options. Two seeds + two thresholds = 255⁴ ≈ 4 billion, but exhaustive search is impossible. Use hill climbing instead:


// start with random (seed, tr)
// for each iteration: try all 255 neighbors (change seed±1 or tr±1)
// keep the best, repeat until no improvement
This finds better solutions in the same search budget.

3. Chain different transform types
Your XOR pass and a delta-encode pass exploit completely different structure:


// delta encode (reversible)
for (int x = size-1; x > 0; x--)
    Data[x] = (Data[x] - Data[x-1] + 256) % 256;
// then apply your PRNG XOR on the delta-encoded data
Delta encoding and XOR create different kinds of collisions and might compound on real data.

The honest ceiling for random data
For rand() % 255 specifically, you're getting entropy reduction from two things: the missing value 255 (slight non-uniformity) and small-sample variance in the 256-byte histogram. These are real but small. On actual data — files, sensor readings, anything with structure — your approach could work dramatically better because the PRNG can align with real patterns in the data rather than just exploiting sampling noise.

Have you tried this on real file data yet? That's where the interesting results would be.
